## Using this component from a code-native integration

Add the component to your integration's `package.json`. The integration must
also depend on `@prismatic-io/spectral`, which this package declares as a peer
dependency; both must resolve to a single copy of spectral.

You do not need to publish the component to Prismatic yourself.
`prism integrations:import` publishes it whenever an integration depends on it
(skipping the publish when nothing has changed). `npm run publish:platform`
publishes it directly.
<% const tarball = `${packageName.replace(/^@/, "").replace("/", "-")}-0.0.1.tgz`; -%>

### From a git repository (no registry needed)

Pin a tag, branch, or commit. npm runs this package's `prepare` script on install,
which builds it, so nothing needs to be committed or published. npm 11 warns that
the script is not covered by `allowScripts`; run
`npm install-scripts approve <%= packageName %>` in the integration to allow it.

GitHub:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= packageName %>": "github:your-org/<%= name %>#v1.0.0"
  }
}
```

GitLab (gitlab.com):

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= packageName %>": "gitlab:your-group/<%= name %>#v1.0.0"
  }
}
```

### From a tarball vendored into the integration

```sh
npm pack                                  # in this directory, writes <%= tarball %>
```

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= packageName %>": "file:./vendor/<%= tarball %>"
  }
}
```

### From a local checkout, while developing

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= packageName %>": "file:../<%= name %>"
  }
}
```

npm builds the linked directory when it installs it, but not after you change
it, so run `npm run build` here after each change. Because the link resolves to
this directory's `node_modules`, make sure both projects use the same
`@prismatic-io/spectral` version.

### From a workspace in an npm monorepo

When the integration and this component live in the same repository, list both
as npm workspaces in the repository's root `package.json`:

```json
{
  "private": true,
  "workspaces": ["components/*", "integrations/*"]
}
```

Then depend on the component by name with an ordinary version range; npm links
the workspace instead of fetching it from a registry:

```json
{
  "dependencies": {
    "<%= packageName %>": "*"
  }
}
```

Run `npm install` from the repository root. npm runs this package's `prepare`
script for the workspace, which builds it, and hoists `@prismatic-io/spectral`
to the root so the integration and the component share one copy, as long as
their spectral ranges agree. After changing the component, rebuild it with
`npm run build --workspace <%= packageName %>`. npm does not support the
`workspace:*` protocol that pnpm, Yarn and Bun use.

